  "verbalization": "Irbesartan is linked to Agtr1 through a series of molecular and pharmacological relationships that are consistent across different pathways. The evidence suggests that Irbesartan, as an angiotensin II receptor antagonist, binds to Agtr1, with the mechanism being supported by its inclusion in the Angiotensin II Receptor Antagonists class. This class includes both Valsartan and Telmisartan, which also bind to Agtr1, indicating a broader pharmacological profile. The lowest common ancestors of Irbesartan and its analogs, such as Forasartan, point to shared structural features that facilitate binding to Agtr1. These findings collectively suggest that Irbesartan exerts its pharmacological effects by interacting with Agtr1, likely through a common mechanism involving angiotensin II receptor antagonism. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of angiotensin II receptor antagonists in modulating Agtr1 activity, reinforcing the connection between Irbesartan and Agtr1.",
